My journey as a demigod did not begin with the typical fancy origin story. The shenanigans that essentially hallmarked a young demigod trying to understand their powers and where they came from. No. My first quest only started at 16, and even then, any sane camper would've told you I was in no state to fight monsters. As I would come to understand very soon, I was a living middle finger to the idea that every single child of a god was going to have the exact same powers and personality. Someone on Mount Olympus was very tired of being shoved into the same old box.

((Back it up. What am I trying to do here? What type of monster is the boy? Eh...whatever he is, he can spit huge wads of incredibly acidic gunk that can melt through steel. He's 16. Ares has violated his oath by not claiming him by 13. Then again, his powers have only just kicked in. Clarisse is having her own issues, as she's been sent to collect this kid convinced he's amnesiac. His journey will be learning to become a warrior, his own way, without being corroded by the expectations his bloodline brings. What is the one thing that will instantly tell the reader who he is? How he utterly refuses to fight until he's backed completely into a corner, only to have all hell break loose when he finally does. Lesson one: It doesn't have to be in order.))
The cold fluorescent lighting of my highschool had always managed to be more than enough to keep me awake. I moved quickly down the hallway. I was short, and I was scrawny, and that meant I was a walking, talking target.
People loved to pick on me. I had learned not to care quite a long time ago.
"Hey! Short stock!" An older boy my mind refused under any circumstances whatever to remember the name of jeered at me. He shoved forward, and I just barely managed to dodge out of the way. Instead, he rammed into the lockers at my left.
*Wait.*
The voice, deep and commanding, resounded from within my own head. I had become accustomed to it, even if I'd told no one about it. What it said usually kept me alive, so I listened. If it said to wait, I waited.
I slowed my pace as the boy turned around to face me.
"Coward." He muttered. He took after me at an insultingly slow jog reserved for an insultingly slow target, i.e. me. I kept walking.
"I don't want to start anything, dude. I promise." I said.
My words fell on deaf ears, just as I knew they would. But I had to try.
He just scoffed.
"Too bad. You're interesting." He said.
/ Interesting? Really? TODO : Kill with fire in future.
I sighed as he went after me. 
A hallway, then another, then another. Dozens of students watching me pass by as the boy pursued.
"Whatcha running away for, boy?" He teased, keeping pace. But something was off about him. Very off.
Nobody focused on me this intensely. Regular bullies got bored of the kid that wasn't reacting the way they wanted and walked away to focus on better targets. But him? He was on me like a hawk, determined to get me.
"You are the one running towards me." I said.
He shrugged and started to run.
Nobody just ran after me. This wasn't normal.
I ran. Left, right, then left again, then two rights. I shoved open the double doors and bolted.
Once we were alone together in the courtyard, I watched him...stop.
*Duck!*
I ducked just in time for...*wait, what?* to fly over my head, embedding itself in the dirt.



I paused. That set of three words burying itself in the back of my mind like a bullet. 
*I have her.*
Her. My mom. I could instantly picture her smiling face, now probably swallowed up in fear and worry. Now it was all real to her. Before, it had probably been storis. Maybe an Iraqi soldier telling tall tales to pass the time, telling fancy war stories about gods and demigods and monsters and giants to impress a pretty girl. Now, a demigod, no, not even that, a *threat*, planned on turning her into his wife to make a point. It wasn't stories anymore. It was a knife against her throat.
"How far to the fortress?" I asked. Clarisse looked at me as if I'd gone properly mad. Fair enough.
"You aren't serious. You're half-dead, in no state to fight, and he *just* wrecked you." She said, trying to talk me out of it.
I simply glared at her.
"You...don't care about any of that. Got it. Four clicks north." She said, taking the hint. "I'm going to tell Chiron you got yourself killed. That you were no help."
"You can tell Chiron any tall tale you want. I'm heading in." I said, and got moving.
Clarisse could, and probably thought about stopping me, but after a few seconds, thought better of it. I *needed* to do this. Prophecy or not, quest or not, they had my mom, so I was *going* to do this.
She wiped the thick mud off her hiking boots and began making the long journey back.
"You're going to have to fight him, you know. You can't talk your way out of this. If you don't want the warrior's life, then this is the end of the road." She called back.
I didn't reply. I didn't really care if Ares helped me or not. I didn't really care about the glory of the quest or the valor that came with winning such an untenable fight. Left. Then right. Over and over again.
Four clicks later, I was staring at the broad side of a fortress. I could literally feel the power radiating from this place like a massive bonfire. Oh, a war had gone down here, all right. A very big one. If this was the power that Samson was tapping into, then I could see why people were convinced he couldn't be beat. His power from this place would've rivaled Ares, at least on one of his sick days. It felt like Ares was standing right here, beckoning me to start the biggest fight I could. And I now knew why his children acted that way. If this was the power they tapped into every day, every time they used their powers, I'd be itching to just punch someone and let loose.
Samson gave me an 'Are you insane, stupid, or both?' look. He wasn't even wearing his armor this time. He clearly didn't think he'd need it with how this fight had gone last time. I still remembered the sensation of the broad side of that blade crashing into my head and bringing me to death's door.
"Where is she?" I grunted.
He grinned.
"Getting familiar with my friends. Don't worry, she's in good hands." He said.
This was where Clarisse had started slashing. This was where it all went wrong.
*A rival his to defeat.*
This was what it meant. Only I could defeat him, because I didn't care about the glory. I wasn't itching to start a fight. I didn't even care about getting his head. This entire mountain was drenched in the power of war, and I was the only demigod at camp who could both lean into that and not get caught up in it. Samson was my match. We were polar opposites. All I'd ever had was my mom, but all he'd ever had was stories of dad. Of the god of war being his father. I was more reserved and cold, and he was far more charming and arrogant and ready to fight.
"Let's try this again. For real this time. No sob stories. No holding back. Get out of the way, or I will get you out of the way." I said.
He cracked up laughing.
"You...you're not serious. Or...oh gods, if you actually are...that would be even more hilarious. What, you think I'm not going to be able to kick your butt just as hard this time around?"
He stopped laughing.
"Well, Hades' buttcrack, you aren't kidding. Fine. I guess I'll let you get one good swing in. To keep things kind of fair." He said, like he was being modest.
He sheathed his massive sword, opening his arms up.
*Ares.* I thought, unsure if he still cared that I existed. I really didn't care either way if he answered. I'd burned that bridge, and I was fine with that.
I waited.
*Yes, son?* He answered.
*You promised me a weapon in my time of need. If you're going to do it, now is the time.*
I closed my eyes, and then...unsheathed the air. I found myself holding a small sword. Maybe a foot long. I flexed my fingers around it.
"Using the Mist to conceal a weapon. Can't honestly say I saw that coming." He said. "It won't matter, though. You have no idea how to use it, or your powers, obviously. And I do."
He gestured me forward.
*War is in your blood, son. Don't think. Act. Let your birthright speak for itself.*

I began circling him.
Then, I imagined my mother's face, contorted in terror, waiting to see what a man arrogant enough to name himself after the strongest man in Jewish mythology without paying any attention to how the story ended would do to her. And suddenly, though I couldn't explain it, the sword just felt *right*.
I didn't need an introduction.
My work would speak for itself.
